{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgBwYmKnjVrb2UfpHzgu1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The O3 framework lazy-loads a React component through `getAsyncLifecycle`. It takes a dynamic import and returns a function that only runs the import when single-spa asks for the lifecycle. The route loader then calls that function when a route or extension is first needed.\n\n**Implementation**\n\n1. `getAsyncLifecycle(lazy, options)` at `openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-react-utils/src/getLifecycle.ts:52-57` returns `() => lazy().then(({ default: Component }) => getLifecycle(Component, options))`. The `import()` runs only when that returned function is called.\n2. `getLifecycle` at `getLifecycle.ts:26-32` wraps the component with `openmrsComponentDecorator` and passes it to `singleSpaReact`. That adds error boundaries, config and extension support.\n3. `getSyncLifecycle` at `getLifecycle.ts:77-79` is the non-lazy counterpart. It wraps an already-imported component in a resolved Promise.\n4. `getAsyncExtensionLifecycle` at `getLifecycle.ts:84` is a deprecated alias for `getAsyncLifecycle`.\n5. `loadLifeCycles(routesAppName, fullComponentName)` at `openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-routes/src/loaders/load-lifecycles.ts:35-58` is the consumer. It parses `appName#componentName` from `routes.json`, loads the app module with `importDynamic`, and calls the named export. That export is the `getAsyncLifecycle` result, so the component's own chunk loads at that point.\n6. `initializeApp` at `load-lifecycles.ts:86-109` runs the module's `startupApp()` once per app before the component is loaded, and caches the promise.\n\n**Documentation**\n\n- `openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-framework/docs/functions/getAsyncLifecycle.md` describes the lazy behaviour and gives the `getAsyncLifecycle(() => import('./root.component'), options)` example.\n- `openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-framework/docs/functions/getSyncLifecycle.md` contrasts it with the synchronous variant.\n- `openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-framework/docs/variables/getAsyncExtensionLifecycle.md` covers the deprecated alias.\n\nI did not read the routes or extensions documentation or the app-side `index.ts` examples, so the way an app exports these lifecycles is inferred from the loader's doc comment, not checked against an example.\n\n```json\n[\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-react-utils/src/getLifecycle.ts\", \"startLine\": 52, \"endLine\": 57},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-routes/src/loaders/load-lifecycles.ts\", \"startLine\": 35, \"endLine\": 58},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-routes/src/loaders/load-lifecycles.ts\", \"startLine\": 86, \"endLine\": 109},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-react-utils/src/getLifecycle.ts\", \"startLine\": 26, \"endLine\": 32},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-react-utils/src/getLifecycle.ts\", \"startLine\": 77, \"endLine\": 79},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-react-utils/src/getLifecycle.ts\", \"startLine\": 84, \"endLine\": 84},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-framework/docs/functions/getAsyncLifecycle.md\", \"startLine\": 1, \"endLine\": 52},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-framework/docs/functions/getSyncLifecycle.md\", \"startLine\": 1, \"endLine\": 52},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-framework/docs/variables/getAsyncExtensionLifecycle.md\", \"startLine\": 1, \"endLine\": 55}\n]\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2080,"cache_read_input_tokens":18762,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2080},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_